414
01:17:12,560 --> 01:17:22,400
matters, it's America, and our companies sell everywhere and therefore Microsoft benefits massively—to a world where it is bipolar? Where Microsoft can't just necessarily have the

415
01:17:22,400 --> 01:17:30,240
right to win all of Europe or India or Singapore. There are actually sovereign AI efforts. What is your thought process here and how do you think about this?

416
01:17:30,240 --> 01:17:48,240
It's a super critical piece. I think that the key, key priority for the US tech sector and the US government is to ensure that we not only do leading innovative work,

417
01:17:48,240 --> 01:18:01,040
but that we also collectively build trust around the world on our tech stack. Because I always say the United States is just an unbelievable place. It's just unique in

418
01:18:01,040 --> 01:18:12,080
history. It's 4% of the world's population, 25% of the GDP, and 50% of the market cap. I think you should think about those ratios and reflect on it.

419
01:18:12,080 --> 01:18:22,240
That 50% happens because quite frankly the trust the world has in the United States, whether it's its capital markets or whether it's its technology and

420
01:18:22,240 --> 01:18:34,880
its stewardship of what matters at any given time in terms of leading sector. If that is broken, then that's not a good day for the United States.

421
01:18:34,880 --> 01:18:47,520
We start with that, which I think President Trump gets, the White House, David Sacks, everyone really, I think, gets it. So therefore I applaud anything

422
01:18:47,520 --> 01:18:59,760
that the United States government and the tech sector jointly does to, for example, put our own capital at risk, collectively as an industry, in every part of the world.

423
01:18:59,760 --> 01:19:10,880
I would like the USG to take credit for foreign direct investment by American companies all over the world. It's the least talked about, but the best

424
01:19:10,880 --> 01:19:21,920
marketing that the United States should be doing is that it's not just about all the foreign direct investment coming into the United States, but the most leading sector, which is these AI factories,

425
01:19:21,920 --> 01:19:33,280
are all being created all over the world. By whom? By America and American companies. And so you start there, and then you even build other agreements around it, which are around

426
01:19:33,280 --> 01:19:49,680
their continuity, their legitimate sovereignty concerns, around whether it's data residency, for them to have real agency and guarantees on privacy, and so on.

427
01:19:49,680 --> 01:20:01,760
In fact, our European commitments are worth reading. We made a series of commitments to Europe on how we will govern our hyperscale investment there

428
01:20:01,760 --> 01:20:10,560
such that the European Union and the European countries have sovereignty. We're also building sovereign clouds in France and in Germany.

429
01:20:10,560 --> 01:20:22,480
We have something called Sovereign Services on Azure, which literally gives people key management services along with confidential computing, including confidential computing in GPUs, which

430
01:20:22,480 --> 01:20:36,800
we've done great innovative work with Nvidia. So I feel very, very good about being able to build, both technically and through policy, this trust in the American tech stack.

431
01:20:36,800 --> 01:20:44,320
How do you see this shaking out as you have this network effect with continual learning and things on the model level? Maybe you have equivalent

432
01:20:44,320 --> 01:20:53,040
things at the hyperscaler level as well. Do you expect that the countries will say, "Look, it's clear one model or a couple models are the best, and so we're going to use them,

433
01:20:53,040 --> 01:21:03,040
but we're going to have some laws around the weights having to be hosted in our country"? Or do you expect that there will be this push so that it has to be a model trained in our country?

434
01:21:03,040 --> 01:21:11,680
Maybe an analogy here is that semiconductors are very important to the economy, and people would like to have their sovereign semiconductors, but TSMC is just better.

435
01:21:11,680 --> 01:21:20,720
And semiconductors are so important to the economy that you will just go to Taiwan and buy the semiconductors. You have to. Will it be like that with AI?

436
01:21:20,720 --> 01:21:33,200
Ultimately, what matters is the use of AI in their economy to create economic value. That's the diffusion theory, which ultimately, it's not the leading sector,

437
01:21:33,200 --> 01:21:42,320
but it's the ability to use the leading technology to create your own comparative advantage. So I think that will fundamentally be the core driver.

438
01:21:42,320 --> 01:21:55,120
But that said, they will want continuity of that. So in some sense, that's one of the reasons why, I believe, there's always going to be a check to "Hey, can this one model have all

439
01:21:55,120 --> 01:22:02,800
the runaway deployment?" That's why open source is always going to be there. There will be, by definition, multiple

440
01:22:02,800 --> 01:22:14,160
models. That'll be one way. That's one way for people to sort of demand continuity and not have concentration risk, that’s another way to say it. And so you say, "Hey, I want multiple models,

441
01:22:14,160 --> 01:22:23,520
and then I want an open source." I feel that as long as that's there, every country will feel like, "Okay, I don't have to worry about deploying the best model

442
01:22:23,520 --> 01:22:35,760
and broadly diffusing because I can always take what is my data and my liquidity and move it to another model, whether it's open source or from another country or what have you."

443
01:22:35,760 --> 01:22:45,120
Concentration risk and sovereignty, which is really agency, those are the two things that will drive the market structure. The thing about this is that

444
01:22:45,120 --> 01:22:57,440
this doesn't exist for semiconductors. All refrigerators, cars have chips made in Taiwan. It didn't exist until now. Even then, if Taiwan is cut off,

445
01:22:57,440 --> 01:23:08,880
there are no more cars or no more refrigerators. TSMC Arizona is not replacing any real fraction of the production. The sovereignty is a bit of a scam, if you will.

446
01:23:08,880 --> 01:23:17,600
It's worthwhile having it, it's important to have it, but it's not real sovereignty. We're a global economy. I think it’s kind of like saying, "Hey,

447
01:23:17,600 --> 01:23:33,680
at this point, we've not learned anything about what resilience means and what one needs to do." Any nation state, including the United States, at this point will do what it

448
01:23:33,680 --> 01:23:46,560
takes to be more self-sufficient on some of these critical supply chains. So I, as a multinational company, have to think about that as a first-class requirement.

449
01:23:46,560 --> 01:23:59,600
If I don't, then I'm not respecting what is in the policy interests of that country long-term. I'm not saying they won't make practical decisions in the short term.

450
01:23:59,600 --> 01:24:12,240
Absolutely, globalization can't just be rewound. All these capital investments cannot be made in a way, at the pace at which… But at the same time, think about it, if somebody showed up in

451
01:24:12,240 --> 01:24:21,600
Washington and said, "Hey, we're not going to build any semiconductor plants," they're going to be kicked out of the United States. The same thing is going to be

452
01:24:21,600 --> 01:24:34,480
true in every other country, too. So therefore we have to, as companies, respect what the lessons learned are, whether it's that the pandemic woke us up or whatever.

453
01:24:34,480 --> 01:24:42,400
But nevertheless people are saying, "Look, globalization was fantastic. It helped supply chains be globalized and be super efficient.

454
01:24:42,400 --> 01:24:54,240
But there's such a thing called resilience, and we want resilience." So therefore that feature will get built. At what pace, I think, is the point you are making. You can't snap your fingers

455
01:24:54,240 --> 01:25:04,000
and say all the TSMC plants now are all in Arizona with all their capability. They're not going to be. But is there a plan? There will be a plan. And should we respect that?

456
01:25:04,000 --> 01:25:16,000
Absolutely. So I feel that that’s the world. I want to meet the world where it is and on what it wants to do going forward, as opposed to saying, "Hey, we have a point of view

457
01:25:16,000 --> 01:25:26,160
that doesn't respect your view." Just to make sure I understand, the idea here is that each country will want some kind of data residency, privacy, et cetera.

458
01:25:26,160 --> 01:25:36,080
And Microsoft is especially privileged here because you have relationships with these countries, you have expertise in setting up these kinds of sovereign data centers.

459
01:25:36,080 --> 01:25:46,720
Therefore Microsoft is uniquely fit for a world with more sovereignty requirements. I don't want to sort of describe it as somehow we're uniquely privileged.

460
01:25:46,720 --> 01:25:56,320
I would just say I think of that as a business requirement that we have been doing all the hard work all these decades, and we plan to. So my answer to Dylan's previous question

461
01:25:56,320 --> 01:26:16,480
was that I take—whether it's in the United States, or when the White House and the USG says, "We want you to allocate more of your wafer starts to fabs in the US"—we take that seriously.

462
01:26:16,480 --> 01:26:28,400
Or whether it is data centers and the EU boundary, we take that seriously. So to me, respecting what are legitimate reasons why countries care about sovereignty,

463
01:26:28,400 --> 01:26:45,200
building for it as a software and a physical plant, is what we'll do. As we go to the bipolar world—US, China—it's not just you versus Amazon,

464
01:26:45,200 --> 01:26:56,080
or you versus Anthropic, or you versus Google. There is a whole host of competition. How does America rebuild the trust? What do you do to rebuild the trust?

465
01:26:56,080 --> 01:27:04,320
To say, "Actually, no, American companies will be the main provider for you." And how do you think about competition with up and coming Chinese companies, whether it

466
01:27:04,320 --> 01:27:15,360
be ByteDance and Alibaba or Deepseek and Moonshot? To add to that question, one concern is how we're talking about how AI is becoming this industrial capex race where you're rapidly having to build

467
01:27:15,360 --> 01:27:25,120
quickly across all loads of supply chain. When you hear that, at least up until now, you just think about China. This is their comparative advantage. And especially if we're not

468
01:27:25,120 --> 01:27:37,200
going to moonshot to ASI next year, but it's going to be decades of buildouts and infrastructure, how do you deal with Chinese competition? Are they privileged in that world?

469
01:27:37,200 --> 01:27:51,040
It’s a great question. In fact, you just made the point of why trust in American tech is probably the most important feature. It's not even the model capability, maybe.

470
01:27:51,040 --> 01:28:04,960
It is, "can I trust you, the company, can I trust you, your country, and its institutions to be a long-term supplier?" That may be the thing that wins the world.

471
01:28:04,960 --> 01:28:17,120
That's a good note to end on. Satya, thank you for doing this. Thank you so much. Thank you. Thank you. It's awesome. You two guys are quite the team.
